{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check each obligation in `requirements.references.definition_globs`.\n\nWhole-name matching, `*` and literal characters:\n- `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` asserts that `foo.*.bar` matches `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`, so `*` crosses dots.\n- The same test asserts that it rejects both `prefix.foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.bar.suffix`, so a glob must match the complete name.\n- It asserts that `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`, and that `foo.bar` does not match `fooxbar`. So `.` is literal, and a literal dot is not a wildcard.\n- It asserts that `_` and `-` match only themselves.\n- `*` matching zero characters is covered through the CLI. `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]` runs `status requirements.*ready` and checks that it lists `requirements.ready` as well as `requirements.xready`.\n\nInvalid globs:\n- The unit test asserts `validate(\"\")` and `validate(\"foo[bar]\")` are errors.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]` checks the empty glob and `foo.[a].bar` as requirements inputs. It checks the error text, for example `definition glob must not be empty`.\n- The CLI test runs `info [` and `status [` and checks for `invalid definition glob`.\n- The authoring tool test `src/authoring/domain_tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_discovery_returns_readable_nodes_without_recording_their_contents]` passes `[bad]` and checks for `invalid definition glob`.\n\nEvery selection surface uses this rule:\n- Requirements evidence inputs are covered by the evidence test, which runs `foo.*.bar` across the dot-separated nested example `foo.a.b.bar` and the requirement `foo.a.bar`.\n- CLI filters are covered by the commands tests above, and by `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]`, which runs `status rules.*` and `info rules.*`.\n- The authoring tool is covered by `requirements.s*`, which selects only the example, and by the invalid-glob case.\n\nBoth kinds:\n- Matching against both requirements and named examples is asserted in the evidence test, which selects an example and a requirement together. It is also asserted in the definitions test, which selects `rules.ready` and `rules.case`.\n- The authoring discovery test selects the example with a glob.\n- The definitions test also checks that a named definition is not selected, and `info rules.used` fails.\n\nEmpty-glob rejection is asserted for the shared validator and for the evidence input path. The CLI and authoring call sites call the same `crate::definition_glob::validate` function and have no conditional of their own, so this gives sufficient coverage.",
  "references": "d09c1fa492d8c1d0cd091d60248c75aedc90ed2fe8e9f901f1aa0891800557d2",
